<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1918–2008</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alexander Solzhenitsyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn was a Russian novelist and historian who spent eight years in Soviet labour camps after criticising Stalin in a private letter. His story One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich (1962) was the first published Soviet account of the camps, and The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> Archipelago, published abroad from 1973, documented the whole system. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1970, was expelled in 1974 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleksandr-solzhenitsyn.jpg" alt="Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bert Verhoeff for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleksandr_Solzhenitsyn_1974crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
